<Guide
  title="Did the Epley Maneuver Work? How to Tell, and What Next"
  description="How to tell if the Epley maneuver worked: which dizziness should stop, which leftover feelings are normal, and when repeating it isn't the answer."
  path="/guides/did-the-epley-maneuver-work/"
  faq={faq}
  sources={sources}
>
  <p class="answer">
    The Epley maneuver worked if the movements that used to set off brief, intense spinning — lying
    down, rolling over in bed, tipping your head back — no longer do. Many people notice this right
    away; for others it takes a few days.<cite><a href="#src-1">[1]</a></cite> A lingering off-balance
    or queasy feeling is common afterwards and doesn't mean it failed. Positional spinning that hasn't
    changed after several attempts does.
  </p>

  <h2>Two different kinds of dizzy</h2>
  <p>
    Most confusion about whether it worked comes from treating all dizziness as the same thing. BPPV
    produces a very specific pattern, and that pattern is the one to judge by.
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th></th><th>Positional spinning (the BPPV)</th><th>Leftover unsteadiness</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Feels like</td><td>The room spins</td><td>Off-balance, woozy, "not quite right"</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Triggered by</td><td>Specific head movements: lying back, rolling to one side, looking up</td><td>Nothing specific, or general movement</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Lasts</td><td>Seconds, then fades while you stay still</td><td>Can hang around for hours or days</td></tr>
      <tr><td>After a successful maneuver</td><td>Gone, or much weaker</td><td>Often still there for a while</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    If the left column has changed, that is the result you were after. The right column is common
    after the crystals have been moved, and nausea is the most frequently reported side effect of the
    maneuver itself.<cite><a href="#src-2">[2]</a></cite> It usually settles on its own
    <mark>[VERIFY]</mark>.
  </p>

  <h2>How clinicians check, and what you can check at home</h2>
  <p>
    In a clinic, success is judged by repeating the positional test and looking for the eye movement
    (nystagmus) that BPPV causes.<cite><a href="#src-2">[2]</a></cite> You can't see your own eyes
    mid-spin, so at home the practical measure is the trigger: does the movement that reliably set it
    off before still do it? Notice it during normal life — getting into bed, rolling over, reaching up
    to a high shelf — rather than repeatedly provoking it to test.
  </p>

  <h2>Why a correctly followed maneuver can still fail</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>The angles weren't reached.</strong> A turn that's short of 45°, or a head that isn't
      tipped back far enough, moves the crystals less effectively. This is the most fixable cause.
      See <a href="/guides/epley-maneuver-angles/">Epley maneuver angles</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Wrong side.</strong> The maneuver is side-specific. If the affected side was guessed
      rather than identified by a clinician, the maneuver may have been done on the wrong ear.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Wrong canal.</strong> Epley is for the posterior canal. The horizontal (lateral) canal
      form uses different maneuvers.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Not BPPV.</strong> Vestibular migraine, vestibular neuritis and other conditions can
      cause dizziness with head movement, and repositioning doesn't help them <mark>[VERIFY]</mark>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>It needs more than one round.</strong> It's common to need to repeat it; Cleveland
      Clinic notes up to three times a day for several days at home.<cite><a href="#src-1">[1]</a></cite>
      Overall it helps about 8 in 10 people.<cite><a href="#src-1">[1]</a></cite>
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Examples</h2>
  <h3>Worked, but still woozy</h3>
  <p>
    The spinning when lying down on the left stops after the second round. For the next two days
    there's a vague unsteadiness walking around, but rolling onto the left side in bed no longer
    triggers anything. That's a success — the positional trigger is gone. The unsteadiness is the
    right-hand column in the table above.
  </p>
  <h3>Partly worked</h3>
  <p>
    The spinning still happens but is shorter and milder. That's usually a reason to repeat the
    maneuver as your clinician advised rather than to give up <mark>[VERIFY]</mark>. It's also the
    case where checking your angles is most useful: a partial result is consistent with positions
    that were close but short.
  </p>
  <h3>Didn't work</h3>
  <p>
    Several careful rounds over a few days, and lying back still sets off the same spinning as
    before. Keep repeating it and you're only repeating the same thing. Stop and see a clinician.
    The side, the canal, or the diagnosis may be different from what you assumed, and the US
    clinical guideline recommends reassessing people whose symptoms don't resolve
    <mark>[VERIFY]</mark>.<cite><a href="#src-3">[3]</a></cite>
  </p>

  <div class="callout">
    <p style="margin-top:0">
      BPPVCoach can't tell you whether the maneuver worked — that's the trigger test above, and your
      clinician. It records a symptom rating before and after each session on your device, so you can
      see whether the positional spinning is changing across attempts, and it shows whether each
      lying position was actually held at the reference angle.
    </p>
